ill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="150" x2="304" y2="156" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="304" y1="90" x2="370" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="304" y1="156" x2="370" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="370" y1="90" x2="370" y2="105" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="370" y1="141" x2="370" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="363" y="105" width="14" height="36" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="165" y="222" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">primary</text><text x="285" y="222" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">secondary</text></svg>', 'A transformer: an a.c. supply across the primary coil and a load across the secondary coil, both wound on an iron core.'),
('dbd42bd3-ac23-5aae-adaa-2aefdd9eb76d', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 27, 27, 'A generator has a peak e.m.f. of 325 V. Calculate its r.m.s. e.m.f., using E_rms = E₀/√2.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '229.8', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E_rms = 325 ÷ √2 = 230 V. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: E_rms = E₀/√2. C1: substitution. B1: the mains value. A1: E_rms = 230 V.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('ad7fe8f2-c965-5cf5-818c-851615b155e2', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 28, 28, 'A doorbell transformer steps 230 V down to 8.0 V. Its primary has 2300 turns. Calculate the number of secondary turns.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '80', 'turns', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Nₛ = Nₚ × Vₛ/Vₚ = 2300 × 8.0 ÷ 230 = 80 turns. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: turns ratio. C1: value. A1: Nₛ = 80 turns.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 450 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgaf078649-t fgaf078649-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgaf078649-t">Doorbell transformer</title><desc id="fgaf078649-d">A transformer with 2300 primary turns connected to 230 V; the secondary gives 8.0 V to a doorbell.</desc><path d="M150,46 a6,6 0 0,1 6,-6 h138 a6,6 0 0,1 6,6 v148 a6,6 0 0,1 -6,6 h-138 a6,6 0 0,1 -6,-6 Z M180,70 h90 v100 h-90 Z" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));fill-rule:evenodd;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="90" x2="184" y2="96" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="100" x2="184" y2="106" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="110" x2="184" y2="116" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="120" x2="184" y2="126" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="130" x2="184" y2="136" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="140" x2="184" y2="146" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="150" x2="184" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="90" x2="100" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="156" x2="100" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="100" y1="90" x2="100" y2="111" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="100" y1="135" x2="100" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="100" cy="123" r="12" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><path d="M93,123 q3.5,-7 7,0 t7,0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="80" y="123" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">230 V</text><line x1="266" y1="90" x2="304" y2="96" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="100" x2="304" y2="106" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="110" x2="304" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="120" x2="304" y2="126" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="130" x2="304" y2="136" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="140" x2="304" y2="146" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="150" x2="304" y2="156" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="304" y1="90" x2="370" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="304" y1="156" x2="370" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="370" y1="90" x2="370" y2="105" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="370" y1="141" x2="370" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="363" y="105" width="14" height="36" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="398" y="123" font-size="14" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">8.0 V</text><text x="165" y="222" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">Nₚ = 2300</text><text x="285" y="222" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">Nₛ = ?</text></svg>', 'A transformer with 2300 primary turns connected to 230 V; the secondary gives 8.0 V to a doorbell.'),
('c2099e7c-c942-5cb6-8c45-6284664d7386', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 29, 29, 'Why are eddy-current brakes used on some roller coasters and trains?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'They brake without contact, so they do not wear and work smoothly even when wet', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The braking force comes from induced currents, with no friction pads. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: no contact. C1: no wear. A1: A (They brake without contact, so they do not wear and work smoothly even when wet).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('568522db-7cba-5515-926b-c0f88dd2c301', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 30, 30, 'In an induction hob, an e.m.f. of 0.50 V is induced around an eddy-current path of resistance 1.0 × 10⁻³ Ω in the pan base. Calculate the power dissipated.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '250', 'W', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'P = E²/R = 0.50² ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 250 W. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: P = E²/R. C1: value. A1: P = 250 W.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('c56aaf45-5447-5e5a-8574-edbf25129138', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 31, 31, 'A generator coil has 100 turns of area 0.0040 m² in a field of 0.20 T. Calculate the angular speed needed for a peak e.m.f. of 12 V.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '150', 'rad s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'ω = E₀/(NBA) = 12 ÷ (100 × 0.20 × 0.0040) = 150 rad s⁻¹. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: E₀ = NBAω. C1: rearranged. C1: value. A1: ω = 150 rad s⁻¹.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('7fe6ac09-01e9-572a-b0be-ce11f1c3b813', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 32, 32, 'Why does a bicycle dynamo become harder to turn when its lamp is switched on?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The current in the dynamo coil produces a force opposing the rotation', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Lenz''s law: the induced current opposes the motion causing it. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: induced current. C1: opposing force. A1: D (The current in the dynamo coil produces a force opposing the rotation).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('febcbb82-68b7-51ab-9a78-14b63f7fda3d', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 33, 33, 'A moving-coil microphone has a 100-turn coil of radius 1.0 cm in a radial field of 0.50 T. The coil moves at 0.010 m s⁻¹. Calculate the e.m.f., in mV, using E = BLv with L the total length of wire.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '31.42 mV', 'mV', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L = 100 × 2π × 0.010 = 6.28 m. E = BLv = 0.50 × 6.283 × 0.010 = 0.0314 V. In mV: 0.03142 × 1000 = 31.4. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: total length. C1: E = BLv. A1: E = 31.4 mV.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ae000416-ff38-571a-85c0-050f0ea6e44a', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 34, 34, 'A workshop draws 50 kW at 230 V through cables of total resistance 0.40 Ω. Calculate the percentage of the power wasted in the cables.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '37.81', '%', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'I = 50 × 10³ ÷ 230 = 217 A. P_loss = (217.4)² × 0.40 = 1.89 × 10⁴ W. Percentage = 1.890 × 10⁴ ÷ 50 × 10³ × 100 = 37.8 %. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: I = P/V. C1: I²R. C1: percentage. A1: loss = 37.8%.', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 182" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg48e8348b-t fg48e8348b-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg48e8348b-t">Workshop supply</title><desc id="fg48e8348b-d">Circuit: a 230 V supply feeding a workshop that draws 50 kW, through cables of total resistance 0.40 Ω.</desc><line x1="26" y1="48" x2="65.2" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="101.2" y1="48" x2="140.4" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="65.2" y="41" width="36" height="14" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="83.2" y="24" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">cables 0.40 Ω</text><line x1="140.4" y1="48" x2="189.2" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="225.2" y1="48" x2="274" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="189.2" y="41" width="36" height="14" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="207.2" y="24" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">workshop 50 kW</text><line x1="26" y1="142" x2="138" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="162" y1="142" x2="274" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="150" cy="142" r="12" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><path d="M143,142 q3.5,-7 7,0 t7,0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="150" y="166" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">230 V</text><line x1="26" y1="48" x2="26" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="274" y1="48" x2="274" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></svg>', 'Circuit: a 230 V supply feeding a workshop that draws 50 kW, through cables of total resistance 0.40 Ω.'),
('4c1f7cf4-26d0-5645-beaa-77c208ba7b4b', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 35, 35, 'Why does the National Grid use alternating rather than direct current?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Transformers need a changing current, and they allow voltages to be stepped up and down efficiently', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Only a changing primary current produces a changing flux in the core. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: transformers need a.c. C1: step up for transmission. A1: B (Transformers need a changing current, and they allow voltages to be stepped up and down efficiently).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('7be7eb8d-54c1-52d2-b802-4b85b474efdf', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 36, 36, 'A wireless charger takes 10 W from the mains and the phone battery receives 7.5 W. Calculate the efficiency.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '75', '%', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Efficiency = 7.5 ÷ 10 × 100 = 75 %. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: output ÷ input. C1: percentage. A1: efficiency = 75%.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('626c8afa-98ee-515b-a4b5-4e49af5e0251', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 37, 37, 'An ideal transformer has Nₛ/Nₚ = 1/20 and draws 0.15 A at its primary. Calculate the secondary current.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3', 'A', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Iₛ/Iₚ = Nₚ/Nₛ, so Iₛ = 0.15 × 20 = 3.0 A. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: current ratio inverse of turns ratio. C1: value. A1: Iₛ = 3.0 A.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 450 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgb3d006e8-t fgb3d006e8-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgb3d006e8-t">Step-down transformer</title><desc id="fgb3d006e8-d">An ideal step-down transformer with Nₛ/Nₚ = 1/20 and a primary current of 0.15 A.</desc><path d="M150,46 a6,6 0 0,1 6,-6 h138 a6,6 0 0,1 6,6 v148 a6,6 0 0,1 -6,6 h-138 a6,6 0 0,1 -6,-6 Z M180,70 h90 v100 h-90 Z" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));fill-rule:evenodd;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="90" x2="184" y2="96" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="100" x2="184" y2="106" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="110" x2="184" y2="116" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="120" x2="184" y2="126" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="130" x2="184" y2="136" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="140" x2="184" y2="146" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="150" x2="184" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="90" x2="100" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="156" x2="100" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="100" y1="90" x2="100" y2="111" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="100" y1="135" x2="100" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="100" cy="123" r="12" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><path d="M93,123 q3.5,-7 7,0 t7,0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="80" y="123" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0.15 A</text><line x1="266" y1="90" x2="304" y2="96" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="100" x2="304" y2="106" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="110" x2="304" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="120" x2="304" y2="126" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="130" x2="304" y2="136" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="140" x2="304" y2="146" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="150" x2="304" y2="156" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="304" y1="90" x2="370" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="304" y1="156" x2="370" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="370" y1="90" x2="370" y2="105" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="370" y1="141" x2="370" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="363" y="105" width="14" height="36" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="165" y="222" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">primary</text><text x="285" y="222" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">secondary</text></svg>', 'An ideal step-down transformer with Nₛ/Nₚ = 1/20 and a primary current of 0.15 A.'),
('bb95ab47-9725-5a8e-85a5-0b7b1cacce3f', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 38, 38, 'How does regenerative braking in an electric car work?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The motor is used as a generator, so the car''s kinetic energy is converted into electrical energy stored in the battery', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The induced current in the motor coils opposes the rotation, slowing the car. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: motor acts as generator. C1: KE to electrical energy. C1: opposing force slows car. A1: C (The motor is used as a generator, so the car''s kinetic energy is converted into electrical energy stored in the battery).', 4, NULL, NULL),
('ab24d3b6-fcee-57e9-92ed-6ae58fad1f7d', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 39, 39, 'A 1200 kg car brakes from 20 m s⁻¹ to rest, and 60 % of its kinetic energy is recovered by regenerative braking. Calculate the energy recovered.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.44e5', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E_k = 0.5 × 1200 × 20² = 2.4 × 10⁵ J. Recovered = 0.60 × 2.4 × 10⁵ = 1.44 × 10⁵ J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: ½mv². C1: × 0.60. A1: E = 1.44 × 10⁵ J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('de6f6c0d-492f-54dc-a5fb-623e83114554', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 40, 40, 'A simple two-pole generator must give a 60 Hz supply. Calculate its rotation rate, in rev min⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3600', 'rev min⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'One revolution per cycle, so 60 revolutions per second. Per minute: 60 × 60 = 3600 rev min⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: f = revolutions per second. C1: × 60. A1: rate = 3600 rev min⁻¹.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('2e09322d-8d50-5fd9-87fb-2260c6aba266', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 41, 41, 'How does a card reader read the magnetic stripe on a bank card?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The magnetised regions moving past a coil in the reading head induce a varying e.m.f.', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Changing flux through the head coil gives the signal. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: moving magnetised stripe. C1: induced e.m.f. in head. A1: A (The magnetised regions moving past a coil in the reading head induce a varying e.m.f.).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('eb1b3773-8a5d-5f10-9fb6-a64b0357b629', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 42, 42, 'A current of 25 A flows along a transmission line of resistance 5.0 Ω. Calculate the voltage drop along the line.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '125', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'V = IR = 25 × 5.0 = 125 V. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: V = IR. C1: value. A1: V = 125 V.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('3ba1918f-2bfd-5d2c-8d84-28c1f9189e04', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 43, 43, 'A power station generates 20 MW at 25 kV. A transformer steps this up to 400 kV for a line of resistance 10 Ω. (a) Calculate the current in the line. (b) Calculate the power lost in the line. (c) Calculate this as a percentage of the power generated. (d) Calculate the percentage lost if the power were sent at 25 kV. Give only the answer to (c), in %.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.125', '%', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) I = 20 × 10⁶ ÷ 400 × 10³ = 50 A. (b) P = 50² × 10 = 2.5 × 10⁴ W. (c) 2.5 × 10⁴ ÷ 20 × 10⁶ × 100 = 0.125 %. (d) I = 800 A; P = 800² × 10 = 6.4 × 10⁶ W, which is 32 %. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: I = P/V. C1: I²R. C1: percentage. C1: 25 kV case. B1: comparison. A1: loss = 0.125%.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 70" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgd7a03aec-t fgd7a03aec-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgd7a03aec-t">Power transmission</title><desc id="fgd7a03aec-d">Block diagram: a power station generating 20 MW at 25 kV feeds a step-up transformer to 400 kV, which feeds a 10 Ω transmission line to the consumers.</desc><rect x="10" y="10" width="88.5" height="50" rx="6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="54.2" y="26" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">power station</text><text x="54.2" y="44" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">20 MW at 25 kV</text><line x1="101.5" y1="35" x2="112.6" y2="35" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="117.5,35 111.2,38 111.2,32" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><rect x="120.5" y="10" width="88.5" height="50" rx="6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="164.8" y="26" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">step-up</text><text x="164.8" y="44" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">transformer</text><line x1="212" y1="35" x2="223.1" y2="35" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="228,35 221.7,38 221.7,32" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><rect x="231" y="10" width="88.5" height="50" rx="6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="275.2" y="26" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">line</text><text x="275.2" y="44" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">400 kV, 10 Ω</text><line x1="322.5" y1="35" x2="333.6" y2="35" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="338.5,35 332.2,38 332.2,32" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><rect x="341.5" y="10" width="88.5" height="50" rx="6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="385.8" y="35" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">consumers</text></svg>', 'Block diagram: a power station generating 20 MW at 25 kV feeds a step-up transformer to 400 kV, which feeds a 10 Ω transmission line to the consumers.'),
('41cf31f3-a3e4-5691-a2fd-4dacc74abf05', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 44, 44, 'A transformer with 4000 primary turns and 200 secondary turns is connected to 230 V. It supplies a 23 W lamp and is 92 % efficient. (a) Calculate the secondary voltage. (b) Calculate the secondary current. (c) Calculate the primary current. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.1087', 'A', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Vₛ = 230 × 200 ÷ 4000 = 11.5 V. (b) Iₛ = 23 ÷ 11.5 = 2.0 A. (c) Input power = 23 ÷ 0.92 = 25 W, so Iₚ = 25 ÷ 230 = 0.109 A. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: Vₛ. C1: Iₛ. M1: input = output ÷ efficiency. C1: Iₚ. A1: Iₚ = 0.109 A.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 450 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="fga81049f1-t fga81049f1-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fga81049f1-t">Transformer and lamp</title><desc id="fga81049f1-d">A transformer with 4000 primary turns connected to 230 V and 200 secondary turns connected to a 23 W lamp.</desc><path d="M150,46 a6,6 0 0,1 6,-6 h138 a6,6 0 0,1 6,6 v148 a6,6 0 0,1 -6,6 h-138 a6,6 0 0,1 -6,-6 Z M180,70 h90 v100 h-90 Z" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));fill-rule:evenodd;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="90" x2="184" y2="96" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="100" x2="184" y2="106" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="110" x2="184" y2="116" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="120" x2="184" y2="126" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="130" x2="184" y2="136" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="140" x2="184" y2="146" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="150" x2="184" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="90" x2="100" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146" y1="156" x2="100" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="100" y1="90" x2="100" y2="111" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="100" y1="135" x2="100" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="100" cy="123" r="12" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><path d="M93,123 q3.5,-7 7,0 t7,0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="80" y="123" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">230 V</text><line x1="266" y1="90" x2="304" y2="96" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="100" x2="304" y2="106" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="110" x2="304" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="120" x2="304" y2="126" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="130" x2="304" y2="136" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="140" x2="304" y2="146" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="266" y1="150" x2="304" y2="156" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="304" y1="90" x2="370" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="304" y1="156" x2="370" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="370" y1="90" x2="370" y2="112" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="370" y1="134" x2="370" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="370" cy="123" r="11" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="362.2" y1="115.2" x2="377.8" y2="130.8" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="362.2" y1="130.8" x2="377.8" y2="115.2" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="390" y="123" font-size="14" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">23 W</text><text x="165" y="222" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">Nₚ = 4000</text><text x="285" y="222" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">Nₛ = 200</text></svg>', 'A transformer with 4000 primary turns connected to 230 V and 200 secondary turns connected to a 23 W lamp.'),
('cea9fb35-c8d4-5ba9-b2d8-451d821f27a9', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 45, 45, 'A generator coil of 250 turns and area 0.012 m² turns at 1500 rev min⁻¹ in a field of 0.080 T. (a) Calculate its angular speed. (b) Calculate the peak e.m.f. (c) Calculate the r.m.s. e.m.f. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '26.66', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ω = 1500 × 2π ÷ 60 = 157 rad s⁻¹. (b) E₀ = 250 × 0.080 × 0.012 × 157.1 = 37.7 V. (c) E_rms = 37.70 ÷ √2 = 26.7 V. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: ω. C1: E₀ = NBAω. C1: substitution. C1: E_rms = E₀/√2. B1: units. A1: E_rms = 26.7 V.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('ac7af545-7ce6-563a-92be-bfd3d50ce4b4', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 46, 46, 'Model the base of a pan on an induction hob as a single conducting loop of radius 0.10 m. The hob produces an alternating field of peak 5.0 mT at 25 kHz through the loop. (a) Show that the peak e.m.f. is πr²B₀ω. (b) Calculate it. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '24.67', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Φ = πr²B₀ sin ωt, so E = πr²B₀ω cos ωt with peak πr²B₀ω. (b) E₀ = π × 0.10² × 5.0 × 10⁻³ × 2π × 25 × 10³ = 24.7 V. Mark scheme (5 marks). M1: flux through loop. B1: peak πr²B₀ω. C1: ω = 2πf. C1: value. A1: E₀ = 24.7 V.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('211cd3d0-f2e1-5415-b605-5522bf7de45f', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 47, 47, 'A wireless charger produces an alternating field of peak 0.20 mT at 100 kHz. The phone''s receiving coil has 20 turns of area 1.0 × 10⁻³ m², face-on to the field. (a) Calculate the angular frequency. (b) Calculate the peak e.m.f. in the phone coil. (c) Explain why a high frequency is used. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.513', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ω = 2π × 100 × 10³ = 6.28 × 10⁵ rad s⁻¹. (b) E₀ = NAB₀ω = 20 × 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 0.20 × 10⁻³ × 6.283 × 10⁵ = 2.51 V. (c) E₀ ∝ ω, so a high frequency gives a useful e.m.f. from a weak field and small coils. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: ω = 2πf. C1: E₀ = NAB₀ω. C1: units converted. C1: value. B1: E₀ ∝ ω. A1: E₀ = 2.51 V.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('a004f5a1-4e90-5d84-b78c-8b3ca8d8f3fe', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 48, 48, 'A 3.0 × 10⁵ kg train slows from 25 m s⁻¹ to 10 m s⁻¹, and 70 % of the kinetic energy lost is returned to the supply by regenerative braking. (a) Calculate the kinetic energy lost. (b) Calculate the energy returned, in MJ. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '55.12 MJ', 'MJ', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ΔE_k = 0.5 × 3.0 × 10⁵ × (25² − 10²) = 7.88 × 10⁷ J. (b) 0.70 × 7.875 × 10⁷ = 5.51 × 10⁷ J. In MJ: 5.512 × 10⁷ ÷ 10⁶ = 55.1. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: ΔE_k. C1: × 0.70. C1: conversion. B1: motor as generator. A1: E = 55.1 MJ.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('436aa71f-efc2-508e-9100-c97c814fd807', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 49, 49, 'A line of resistance 8.0 Ω must carry 50 MW with no more than 1.0 % of the power lost. (a) Calculate the maximum power loss allowed. (b) Calculate the maximum current. (c) Calculate the minimum transmission voltage, in kV. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '200 kV', 'kV', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P_loss = 0.010 × 50 × 10⁶ = 5.0 × 10⁵ W. (b) I = √(5.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 8.0) = 250 A. (c) V = P/I = 50 × 10⁶ ÷ 250.0 = 2.00 × 10⁵ V. In kV: 2.000 × 10⁵ ÷ 1000 = 200. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: allowed loss. C1: I = √(P/R). C1: V = P/I. C1: conversion. B1: need for step-up transformer. A1: V = 200 kV.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('a1bd338d-ee1f-584f-a4e6-068d9fe1b6de', 'f7ead7fd-7729-4ec2-9a2e-7a00cfac7c96', '24d540aa-65ef-46d8-ac39-5c62ee4aed70', 'a-level', '26.5', '9702 coursebook 26.5 · Everyday examples of electromagnetic induction', 50, 50, 'In a guitar pickup coil of 5000 turns, the vibrating string changes the flux through each turn by 2.0 × 10⁻⁸ Wb in 1.0 ms. (a) Calculate the average e.m.f., in mV. (b) Explain why the output alternates at the frequency of the note. Give only the answer to (a).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '100 mV', 'mV', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = NΔΦ/Δt = 5000 × 2.0 × 10⁻⁸ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 0.10 V. In mV: 0.10 × 1000 = 100. (b) The flux increases and decreases as the string vibrates, so the e.m.f. reverses each half cycle of the vibration. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: E = NΔΦ/Δt. C1: substitution. C1: conversion. B1: alternating flux change. A1: E = 100 mV.', 5, NULL, NULL)
